<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 74 0 R/T(Retry/abort and wait-for-cycle interpretation: the lock schedule itself waits rather than aborting on ordinaryconflicts. Canonical lock ordering yields zero lock-order deadlock cycles in this model. Basic TO has no waits; a conservative overlap-based retry indicator can be calculated from the same request stream, but it is not a measured SQL-engine abort count. Recovery work is modeled as at most log-rate \327 checkpoint interval records; it is not an observed disk replay count. )/E(Retry/abort and wait-for-cycle interpretation: the lock schedule itself waits rather than aborting on ordinaryconflicts. Canonical lock ordering yields zero lock-order deadlock cycles in this model. Basic TO has no waits; a conservative overlap-based retry indicator can be calculated from the same request stream, but it is not a measured SQL-engine abort count. Recovery work is modeled as at most log-rate \327 checkpoint interval records; it is not an observed disk replay count. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

392 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 74 0 R/T(Recommendation: use strict two-phase locking for movements, with canonical ascending lock acquisition, FIFO lock queues, a unique request ID, and a consistent-snapshot report. Use WAL immediate update with commit-force and clean checkpoints. This choice prioritizes correctness and simple recovery. Serializability follows from 2PL; strictness follows because X locks remain held until commit/abort. These are separate properties: 2PL provides conflict serializability, while strict 2PL provides strict schedules. )/E(Recommendation: use strict two-phase locking for movements, with canonical ascending lock acquisition, FIFO lock queues, a unique request ID, and a consistent-snapshot report. Use WAL immediate update with commit-force and clean checkpoints. This choice prioritizes correctness and simple recovery. Serializability follows from 2PL; strictness follows because X locks remain held until commit/abort. These are separate properties: 2PL provides conflict serializability, while strict 2PL provides strict schedules. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

393 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 74 0 R/T(Theory predicts that independent pool accesses can proceed concurrently, while requests to one exclusive pool serialize. The uniform workload spreads requests over 20 pools and therefore has shorter waits. Under the 81% skew, pool 20 receives most requests; its single-record service capacity is 1/0.020 = 50 deliveries/s. Since 0.81\32767 = 54.27 requests/s target that pool, demand exceeds its service capacity and a queue grows with burst size. )/E(Theory predicts that independent pool accesses can proceed concurrently, while requests to one exclusive pool serialize. The uniform workload spreads requests over 20 pools and therefore has shorter waits. Under the 81% skew, pool 20 receives most requests; its single-record service capacity is 1/0.020 = 50 deliveries/s. Since 0.81\32767 = 54.27 requests/s target that pool, demand exceeds its service capacity and a queue grows with burst size. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

394 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 74 0 R/T(The simulator reflects this: the hotspot workload has higher mean and tail latency and lower completed throughput for large bursts. This is not proof that TO or any lock implementation will have the same numbers; real performance depends on disk, CPU, worker count, lock manager, batching and transaction duration. The model intentionally gives the same operation cost and arrival stream to the compared workload modes. )/E(The simulator reflects this: the hotspot workload has higher mean and tail latency and lower completed throughput for large bursts. This is not proof that TO or any lock implementation will have the same numbers; real performance depends on disk, CPU, worker count, lock manager, batching and transaction duration. The model intentionally gives the same operation cost and arrival stream to the compared workload modes. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

395 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 74 0 R/T(Dynamic locks can deadlock when resources are acquired in inconsistent order; canonical ordering eliminates that specific cycle. TO avoids lock deadlocks but can restart older transactions. The experiment's lock wait is a resource queue, not a deadlock. Admission control can bound in-flight work, but may move waiting from lock queues to an admission queue rather than remove it. )/E(Dynamic locks can deadlock when resources are acquired in inconsistent order; canonical ordering eliminates that specific cycle. TO avoids lock deadlocks but can restart older transactions. The experiment's lock wait is a resource queue, not a deadlock. Admission control can bound in-flight work, but may move waiting from lock queues to an admission queue rather than remove it. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

