803 0 obj
<</Title(Order-preserving interleaving counts: for two transactions with m and noperations, the number of schedules that preserve each transaction's internal order is C\(m+n,m\). For the selected4-operation movement and 3-operation report, C\(7,4\)=35 possible interleavings. If two 4-operation movements compete,C\(8,4\)=70. These are combinatorial counts, not counts of only valid schedules.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 791 0 R/Prev 802 0 R>>
endobj

804 0 obj
<</Title(Schedule pattern)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 792 0 R/Next 817 0 R>>
endobj

805 0 obj
<</Title(Basic TO only orders reads and writes by timestamps. To make TO strict, delay a read/write of an item while a younger transaction has an uncommitted write to it, or use a commit-order/strict-TO rule. This prevents dirty reads and dirty overwrites and makes rollback local.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 792 0 R/Prev 818 0 R>>
endobj

806 0 obj
<</Title(TASK 5 \205 Analyse Locking and Deadlock Handling \(3 Marks\))/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 792 0 R/First 819 0 R/Last 820 0 R/Count -4/Next 821 0 R>>
endobj

807 0 obj
<</Title(TASK 10 \205 Compare Theory with Experimental Behaviour \(4 Marks\))/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 822 0 R/First 823 0 R/Last 824 0 R/Count -3/Next 793 0 R>>
endobj

808 0 obj
<</Title(Recommendation: use strict two-phase locking for movements, with canonical ascending lock acquisition, FIFO lock queues, a unique request ID, and a consistent-snapshot report. Use WAL immediate update with commit-force and clean checkpoints. This choice prioritizes correctness and simple recovery. Serializability follows from 2PL; strictness follows because X locks remain held until commit/abort. These are separate properties: 2PL provides conflict serializability, while strict 2PL provides strict schedules.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 793 0 R/First 825 0 R/Last 826 0 R/Count -6>>
endobj

809 0 obj
<</Title(The changed rule concentrates 81% of allocations on one canal pool and adds a per-farm daily ceiling. The previous assumption that resource accesses are dispersed or that the transaction touches only a fixed pair of canal records is no longer sufficient. The dominant pool receives 54.27 requests/s at 67 requests/s total. With 20 ms exclusive service, its theoretical single-record capacity is 50 requests/s, so its utilization is 108.5% and backlog grows during sustained input. The 35-second restart deadline must be checked against retained log volume. At 1,000 log records/s, a 285-second interval retains 285,000 records; under the stated 0.02 ms/record replay and 2-second startup model this is 7.7 seconds, below 35 seconds. However, it has less margin than a 20-second interval \(20,000 records; 2.4 seconds\). These are model estimates, not guarantees.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 794 0 R>>
endobj

