820 0 obj
<</Title(Wait-die: older requester may wait for younger holder; younger requester aborts if it requests a lock held by older. Wound-wait: older requester aborts \(wounds\) younger holder; younger requester waits for older. Starvation can occur through repeated victimization. FIFO queues, bounded retries and retry aging \(increasing priority after each retry\) reduce risk, but do not prove fairness under every workload.)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 806 0 R/Prev 836 0 R>>
endobj

821 0 obj
<</Title(TASK 6 \205 Analyse Timestamp-Based Concurrency Control \(3 Marks\))/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 806 0 R/First 837 0 R/Last 838 0 R/Count -3/Next 839 0 R>>
endobj

822 0 obj
<</Title(TASK 9 \205 Record Experimental Performance \(3 Marks\))/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 840 0 R/First 841 0 R/Last 842 0 R/Count -2/Next 807 0 R>>
endobj

823 0 obj
<</Title(Theory predicts that independent pool accesses can proceed concurrently, while requests to one exclusive pool serialize. The uniform workload spreads requests over 20 pools and therefore has shorter waits. Under the 81% skew, pool 20 receives most requests; its single-record service capacity is 1/0.020 = 50 deliveries/s. Since 0.81\32767 = 54.27 requests/s target that pool, demand exceeds its service capacity and a queue grows with burst size.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 807 0 R/Next 843 0 R>>
endobj

824 0 obj
<</Title(Dynamic locks can deadlock when resources are acquired in inconsistent order; canonical ordering eliminates that specific cycle. TO avoids lock deadlocks but can restart older transactions. The experiment's lock wait is a resource queue, not a deadlock. Admission control can bound in-flight work, but may move waiting from lock queues to an admission queue rather than remove it.)/Dest[17 0 R/Fit]/Parent 807 0 R/Prev 843 0 R>>
endobj

825 0 obj
<</Title(Use a single atomic transaction for source debit, destination credit and request-ID insertion.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 808 0 R/Next 844 0 R>>
endobj

826 0 obj
<</Title(A report must cite the actual throughput/latency values from the Task 9 table for the chosen workload. Under the declared assumptions, the 81% hotspot is the limiting access path. If the farm ceiling is added, lock the farm/day guard and canal pool records in one global order, or use serializable predicate protection for the farm/day allocation query.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 808 0 R/Prev 845 0 R>>
endobj

827 0 obj
<</Title(Strict 2PL remains correct but the hotspot causeswaits and longer tails. The old 285-second checkpoint interval passes the stated simplified restart equation, but shouldnot be accepted as a hard 35-second guaranteewithout measured worst-case log generation, redo/undo throughputand checkpoint duration. The skew results showthat queueing, not just protocol choice, dominates the hotspot.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 810 0 R/First 846 0 R/Last 847 0 R/Count -3>>
endobj

828 0 obj
<</Title(Decision: MODIFY, not retain unchanged and not replace the core concurrency protocol. Keep strict 2PL + WAL, but extend the lock scope to include \(1\) source canal pool, \(2\) destination canal pool, \(3\) farm_id + service_date usage row, and \(4\) any predicate/index range used to locate the farm's daily allocations. Acquire all lock keys in one deterministic global order. Validate remaining farm allowance and source pool balance after locks are held, then debit/credit and increment daily farm usage in the same transaction.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 811 0 R/Next 829 0 R>>
endobj

829 0 obj
<</Title(Tested change: the supplied model tests uniform versus 81% hot-pool access and measures the resulting resource queue. Proposed domain extension: farm/day predicate validation and bounded admission are design requirements, not implemented or experimentally verified by the token-only simulation. Do not claim a throughput improvement from these proposals. Use a 20-second checkpoint as the initial modeled policy and validate it with a crash-recovery benchmark.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 811 0 R/Prev 828 0 R>>
endobj

